\f4\fs26 Hayley had heard enough and sent the team in. They blew the door, threw in concussion grenades and star-fire flares before they poured in. Haloren Smythe was out cold, but Cracker was wandering about coughing and spluttering. \
\'93What took you?\'94 Cracker yelled as Detective Lanning entered the room. \'93Cut it a bit fine didn\'92t you? He could have killed me when I was unconscious and you never would have known.\'94\
\'93I needed the confession for the Marrin murder otherwise you would not be off the hook.\'94 She thought her judgment had been timely and divisive. \
\'93Come to think of it, you were pretty slow on the draw when Agent Jones was about to kill me, too.\'94 He did not know if it were true, but the thought of being expendable did not sit well with him and if his concerns were valid, it exposed her as a stone-cold bitch. \
\'93You are only imaging things. After today you can get your old life back.\'94 She appeared cheerful but inwardly she was, momentarily, second guessing her operational acumen. 
\f5\i Was I too slow? It doesn\'92t matter, he is only level 2.
\f4\i0  \'93I have to get this one back to headquarters.\'94\
\'93No, wait. You can interview me here, but don\'92t take me anywhere.\'94 Halo coughed out as the uniforms in flack jackets finalised trussing him up. Hayley had no problem speaking with him there and then. There will be that much more to talk about when she had him strapped in the interrogation suite. \'93You have got this all wrong. I have been protecting Cracker for years.\'94\
\'93Can you tell me about that?\'94 Sarah was unconvinced. 
\f5\i This low-life will say anything to sleaze his way out of this one.
\f4\i0 \
\'93Yes, but I need certain incentives.\'94 Halo thought to press for concessions. As a member of the upper elite he simply could not help himself to negotiate for more and more. If he could gain some concessions from her, it followed that he could gain more.\
\'93You will be offered neither promises or threats. I am investigating multiple murders and you will receive no favours from me.\'94 She shut him down quickly and without hesitation.\
\'93I at least need protection.\'94\
\'93You will be afforded every protection under the law.\'94 She was genuine in her promise to protect her prisoner. She had a duty of care and would not shirk her responsibility.\
\'93You may find what I am about to say difficult to believe. Anthony Hardy, Cleaner, L.2 is the great-grandson of Jameson Hardy, Corporate Magnate. Not only that, as far as I can tell, he is the only living direct descendant.\'94 \
\'93Oh, come on!\'94 Cracker burst out laughing . \'93You can do better than that.\'94\
 \'93It is a fact, Anthony. Your grandfather, may the market be merciful to his memory, would fuck the hair on a barber shop floor. He had quite a number of children, all outside of wedlock, scattered throughout Ulyssia to various different mothers. When he was declared dead, his relatives and his heirs, were hunted down and murdered. A small group, my father being one, all prodigies of Jamison Hardy were tasked to protect the last few survivors.\'94\
\'93Why would you do that? His fortune must have evaporated by now.\'94 Hayley did not sound impressed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\qc\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1 * * *
\f2 \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\pardeftab720\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f4\fs26 \'93Not all of the accounts. There exists off-shore accounts, vast sums in various tax havens, that cannot be accessed by anyone other than Jameson Hardy or his heir. The next closest relative is determined by examination of the applicants DNA, not fancy documentation and no-one, to this day, has managed to lay claim to the money and live long enough to spend it. On each and every occasion the banks have been approached by a claimant, they are at once cut down by a coparcener or the enemies of Jamison Hardy. The wealthiest in the world have hatched schemes for decades trying to access the money hidden away, but they have always failed. There are trillions waiting to be liberated and this lump of human refuse sitting here before you is the only person in the world who can access the accounts.\'94\
\'93If you were protecting Cracker, why did you kill so many innocent girls?\'94 Hayley refused to buy this story.\uc0\u8232 \
\'93Innocent girls? There is no such thing! Anyone that came close to Cracker was taken out. Most of them were genuine targets, obviously agents sent to kill him, but perhaps a few were collateral damage.\'94 He knew there was more that just a few, but fuck them, they had got in his way, a crime sufficient to attract summary execution. \'93Who do you think the target was for the Stick-it-Inn bombing?\'94\
\'93Me?\'94 Cracker was still in denial. \'93Am I so important that hundreds of other are killed just to get to me? No, it is not possible.\'94\
\'93But the murders were so vicious, so brutal. The violence displayed and the damage inflicted on your victims was more, much more, than required to repel an attack. The level of hate towards women was hideous \'94 Hayley brought the conversation back to the topic at hand.\
\'93What is the difference between killing a man and killing a woman? They bleed, feel pain and die in pretty much the same fashion as men. There is nothing so special about woman kind that affords them any protection from me. Are you suggesting that killing men is preferable? Or desirable? That the human condition of the male is so objectionable, it is simply okay to kill them on that basis alone?\'94 Halo was an equal opportunity killer. \'93You will probably never know or appreciate the precision, the skill and dedication required to snuff out life, time and time again. It becomes quite addictive and for better or worse, I embraced it, reveled in it. I will kill anyone that gets in my way.\'94 He was glad he could get some of this off his chest. He did not feel guilty, not at all. He had been busting to share his love of killing with someone, anyone. He had never expected, however, he would divulge the truth of his heartfelt passion to a cop. He had considered the possibility that he might, one day, be caught and interviewed, but was sure his social status would afford protection. Failing that, he dreamed of being brought down in a hail of police plasma fire in a brave and spectacular last stand. The concussion grenades Hayley\'92s team had used knocked him out cold, denied him that glory.\
\'93How many heirs were under protection?\'94 The number of relatives of Jameson Hardy must run into the hundreds, she thought.\
\'93I don\'92t know exactly. There were numerous cells that operate independently, but have diminished over the years as each heir was murdered. Jamison was perfectly aware his progeny were under threat and even while he was still alive, plots were being hatched and alliances forged. All for the one purpose of wrestling away his hidden wealth. Publicly acknowledging any of his numerous descendants would have placed them in mortal danger. He concluded it was safer to leave them to their own devices, offering up no overt assistance, just covert protection teams.\'94\
\'93You were never my friend were you? Not like the others, they were real friends.\'94\
\'93No, I was never your friend.\'94 Haloren replied with no shame. He had a job to do and emotional attachments would prove counter-productive. \
\'93My real friends are all dead now.\'94\
\'93No, you never had friends. Major, Abe and any other acquaintances you have ever had, worked for me. You have been under observation and our protection for most of your life. The only reason you are still alive is because of me and my company of dedicated defenders.\'94\
\'93Bullshit.\'94 Cracker found it hard to believe that his shit-filled existence was, in reality, charmed and he had reason to be grateful. By any objective measure his life had been one of hardship and deprivation and he firmly believed he had solid grounds for complaint. \
 \'93Who do you think brought you up?\'94 Halo looked Cracker up and down and then smiled at Detective Lanning.\
\'93My uncle, Chester. At least that\'92s what he told me.\'94\
\'93Indeed, that is what he told you. He was not your uncle, but one of the first to join the team.\'94\
\'93But he abandoned me when I was 16 years old. Fucking prick went out one day and never came back.\'94\
\'93That fucking prick, as you call him, was tortured and killed that day and the fact that you are still alive means he kept his mouth shut. You have much to be grateful for.\'94\
\'93I didn\'92t know that and could not know that, but I was left high and dry while not yet a man. I didn\'92t know when I was to be kicked out onto the street and have spent all the years since frightened at the prospect of being homeless. I have never paid rent, I don\'92t know where or who to pay. You are level 6 and could never imagine the creeping sickness of that constant, unrelenting anxiety, not knowing what would happen from to me one day to the next.\'94\
\'93No one knows that, you idiot. There is no such thing as certainty, just the promise of it. Don\'92t you know that?\'94 The great teacher needed an education. \'93You lazy minded, useless level 2 turd. Have you ever bothered to read the documents Chester left for you. They were sent shortly after of he died. Did you read them?\'94\
\'93No. I couldn\'92t be bothered. As far as I was concerned he abandoned me and I wasn\'92t interested in anything he had to say. (????) He could go fuck himself.\'94\
\'93Idiot. You own the apartment. I could never understand why you didn\'92t sell up and improve your station. I guess being a complete moron slowed you down.\'94 Haloren was incensed. 
\f5\i I have been protecting this imbecile for years. What a fucking waste of time, unless of course I can get hold of some of that cash. The ultimate prize.
\f4\i0 \
\'93Haloren Smythe, Chief Executive Officer, L.6?\'94 Hayley had sat back and allowed the conversation to unfold naturally and was pleasantly surprised by her suspects candor. \'93You are under arrest for the murders of James Marrin and Andrea Novetine.\'94\
\'93No, I cannot be taken to custody. A member of the Upper Executive Class locked up for murder? One of two things will happen. I will either be seen as an embarrassment to my peers, in which case I will be killed in short shrift or I will be protected, no-matter what you throw at me. I will never spend one minute in gaol. Which scenario do you favour? Neither will further the interests of justice and I know you are a true servant of the law.\'94\
\'93I have in the recent past, bent the rules, but that was a very special case and I do not propose repeating that again. Your case is less than exceptional and on this occasion I will stick to procedure. You will be charged and processed like any other prisoner. What will happen to you will be out of my control.\'94 She did not care if he died in custody or his benefactors busted him out, just as long as her reputation remained unsullied. The Nightclub Vampire was finally in custody and she was taking him in.\
\'93Well, fuck you then. I will say nothing further to you.\'94 He said not another word.
